<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bingen am Rhein</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bingen am Rhein is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahe">Nahe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, where the Rhine Gorge begins. A Roman fort and later a possession of the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, it passed to the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhenish-hesse">Rhenish Hesse</a> in 1816. It is associated with the abbess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildegard-of-bingen">Hildegard of Bingen</a>, who founded her convent on the nearby Rupertsberg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bingen-am-rhein.jpg" alt="Bingen am Rhein" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fourdee at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bingen_above.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Congress of Vienna redrew his borders: in 1816 he ceded Westphalia to Prussia and Hesse-Homburg was restored as a separate landgraviate, while he received Rhenish Hesse on the left bank of the Rhine, with Mainz, Worms, <b>Bingen</b> and Alzey.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 18 March 1793 the Rhenish-German National Convention proclaimed a free state covering the left bank of the Rhine from Landau to <b>Bingen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-mainz">Republic of Mainz</a></li></ul></section>
